				\begin{document}$$Z=0.49,p=0.62;95\%\text{ CI }[116.80, 146.39]$$\end{document}Z=0.49,p=0.62;95%CI[116.80,146.39]).Fig. 4
Average test–retest consistency of synesthetes’ colored trials (leftmost bar), synesthetes’ “no color” trials (middle bar), and non-synesthetes (rightmost bar). Color associations (and thus test–retest consistency) for synesthetes’ “no color” trials (middle bar) are not typically collected in most synesthesia experiments. The dotted line (y = 135.3) reflects the test–retest consistency threshold which optimally separates English-speaking synesthetes and non-synesthetes (Rothen et al., 2013; but see Root et al., 2025).

The surprising consistency of synesthetes’ “no color” trials suggests that synesthetes and non-synesthetes might differ even on only “no color” trials (i.e., removing trials in which the participant reported the subjective experience of a color). Indeed, synesthetes’ “no color” trials are sufficient to classify synesthesia with above-chance accuracy (DeLong Test, \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
